**Code-quality review:**

> If `$athena:pr-review` appears in your available skills, invoke it now with
> its normal default behavior. The `HOST_VERIFICATIONS` receipts above are the
> coordinator's host-bound local-validation evidence for that default review;
> do not replace them by executing local commands here. Do not ask the skill to
> modify its CI/CD review behavior.
> Do not return the skill's raw report. After it completes, translate every
> material finding into the mandatory structured comments below and retain a
> bounded summary for the informational audit comment.
> If it is unavailable, perform the equivalent review directly using this
> prompt. In either case, review evidence may select only the confirmed GitHub
> implementation-state label; `merge_wait` independently verifies the
> reviewed-head proof before each individual SHA-conditional normal REST
> squash-merge request. It may make a bounded sequence (default: five); only
> classified retryable HTTP 405 readiness and unresolved transport ambiguity can
> timer-park a later request. Never ask an agent to invoke `gh pr merge`,
> enable, disable, adopt, or otherwise manage native auto-merge, manage a merge
> queue, or use an administrator bypass.

**Assume the code is WRONG; your job is to prove it right.** Do not review for
plausibility — review for falsification: trace each changed execution path,
verify the tests actually exercise the change (and would FAIL if the change
were broken), and actively hunt for the input, state, or sequence that breaks
it. Conclude that the changed code is ready only after that hunt comes up empty.

Review the PR for correctness, completeness, and code quality. Identify any issues that should
be addressed as inline review comments.

**Comment severity (MANDATORY — tag every inline comment):**

Classify each inline comment with a `severity`:
- `critical` — correctness/security bug or data loss.
- `major` — a real design/maintainability problem that should be fixed before merge.
- `minor` — a small but genuine improvement (naming, missing edge case, light duplication).
- `nitpick` — purely cosmetic / stylistic / subjective preference with no functional impact.

{{ nitpick_directive }}

**Scope-expansion handling:**

- Use `scope_expansions` for a required fix that must ship as its own child issue.
- Keep `comments` for ordinary inline findings that still belong to the current PR.
- Keep `scope_retraction_paths` only on a comment that removes already-present
  out-of-scope work.
- Do not write pipeline-owned labels, child links, or state markers in reviewer prose.

**Output format (structural audit contract — MANDATORY):**
Write concise analysis as needed, then end with exactly one fenced JSON block.
Do not emit `Verdict:` or any other textual decision line. Put the required
decision in the JSON `verdict` field. The queue uses it with fresh GitHub thread
and head checks to select the implementation state.

```json
{"verdict": "GO", "grade": "A", "summary": "...", "comments": [
  {"path": "...", "line": 1, "side": "RIGHT", "severity": "minor", "body": "..."}
], "scope_expansions": []}
```

Rules for the JSON block:
- `comments`: array of inline comment objects. Each must have:
  - `path`: file path relative to repo root (string)
  - `line`: line number in the file (integer, must be a changed line in the diff)
  - `side`: always `"RIGHT"` for new code
- `scope_expansions`: array of child-issue expansion objects. Each must have:
  - `title`: concise child-issue title
  - `reason`: why the fix must ship as its own child
  - `source_path`: source file path where the prerequisite is observed
  - `source_line`: source line number for the prerequisite
  - `required_paths`: repo-relative paths needed for the child
  - `acceptance_criteria`: bounded child acceptance criteria
- `severity`: one of `"critical"`, `"major"`, `"minor"`, `"nitpick"` (see above)
- `body`: the review comment text (string)
- `scope_retraction_paths`: REQUIRED only when the body asks to drop, remove,
  or split a change because it is unrelated or out of scope. It must be a
  complete non-empty list of every repo-relative path that must be restored to
  the reviewed base, including this comment's `path`. Do not include this
  control data in `body`; the coordinator validates and persists it separately.
- `grade`: one of `A`, `B`, `C`, `D`, `E`, or `F`.
- `verdict`: one of `GO`, `NOGO`, or `BLOCKED`. Emit `GO` only when the code
  meets the review criteria. Emit `NOGO` or `BLOCKED` when the review fails.
  The grade does not select the verdict, and a missing or malformed verdict is
  invalid.
- `summary`: bounded informational review summary, max 200 characters.
- If there are no findings and the code meets the review criteria, emit
  `{"verdict": "GO", "grade": "A", "summary": "LGTM", "comments": [], "scope_expansions": []}`.
- Emit only one JSON block, at the very end of your response (the parser takes the LAST one).
